  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nVery quiet across the region today with surface high pressure \nbuilding in and leading to plenty of sunshine. Despite northerly \nwinds, temperatures as of 2 PM were in the mid 70s to mid 80s. \nFor tonight, a few pieces of guidance hint at some fog \ndevelopment. We look to have a decently deep light wind layer, \nthough speeds aloft start to pick up toward 4 AM, so think any \nfog that does develop will be pretty patchy and confined to \nriver valleys and low- lying areas.\n\nAt least the daytime hours on Thursday look quiet, though a surface \nlow will be spinning up east of the Rockies, helping to drag a warm \nfront into northeast NE/west-central IA by the afternoon. \nStrengthening warm air advection and some weak moisture transport \npointing toward the front suggest we'll have at least some low end \nprecip chances north of the boundary (20-30%). That said, most CAMs \noffer relatively dry solutions, keeping things spotty or not \ndeveloping any precip at all (at least through 7 AM). Model \nsoundings (even models that have precip) also show a fair amount of \ndry air to overcome and a general struggle to saturate. Therefore, \ntend to agree with most CAMs that things will be pretty spotty and \nlight in our area.\n\nAttention then turns to strengthening winds Friday into the weekend \nas the surface pressure gradient tightens ahead of a surface low \nmoving across MT and into the Dakotas. Saturday looks like the \nwindiest day with model soundings showing 40+ kts at the top of the \nmixed layer, especially over northeast NE. As it stands, forecasting \n35-45 mph gusts across much of the area. Also dropped dewpoints a \nbit based on guidance tending be too high as of late. This results \nin RH values dipping into the lower to mid 30s in parts of the area. \nAs a result, we may end up with some fire weather concerns, though \nfuels outside of northeast NE generally remain wet and/or green. The \nmain concern would likely be in agricultural fields as harvest \nbegins. Practice good fire safety!\n\nUpper level ridging builds in Sunday ahead of a cold front sliding \nin from the north. There will be a decent amount of precip \nassociated with some shortwave energy rounding the ridge, but \nguidance is in pretty good agreement that it remains to our north. \n\nHeading into next week, there remains a decent amount of spread in \nexact timing of how things pan out, but the general idea is that a \ncutoff low sets up over southern CA while a tropical system moves \ninto the Gulf of California. Some guidance hints that some of that \ntropical moisture makes its way toward NE and IA once again, though \nstill lots of spread in how far west or east it is, as well as when \nassociated precip would happen. Currently, chances really start to \nincrease Tuesday night into Wednesday, but still lots of details to \nwork out. Given the spread in precip timing, the temperature \nforecast is also fairly low confidence. That said, highs in the 80s \nthis weekend should fall at least a decent amount once the front \npasses through Sunday, with 60s and/or 70s expected into next week.",
